    "offer_browser_tts": "true",
    "teacher_markdown": "**Intent:** Precision without jargon. Students learn that pointing at a specific part of the page (\"the heading that says...\", \"the section about...\") plus a concrete outcome is what makes AI accurate - no HTML vocabulary required. The AI on this level is configured to ask a clarifying question when the target is ambiguous, so vague prompts produce a conversation instead of a guess.\r\n\r\n**Circulation questions:**\r\n\r\n- \"Read me your prompt. Which words point at the part? Which words describe the outcome?\"\r\n- \"What happened when you were vague?\"\r\n\r\n**Expected mistakes and misconceptions:**\r\n\r\n- Students may name the outcome but not the target (\"make it blue\" - which *it*?) - have them re-read their prompt looking for the pointing words\r\n- Students may reach for technical-sounding words they don't know - reassure them plain language works: \"say it the way you'd say it to a friend\"\r\n",
    "require_edit_to_continue": "true",
    "display_name": "Point at What You Mean"
  },
